 Even when all clients optimize the same objective function, constant-step stochastic updates can produce a systematic parameter displacement. The global model continues to fluctuate, and its expected parameters under the stationary distribution may differ from the objective minimizer. This difference is the \emph{stationary mean bias}. Increasing the number of clients reduces the variance of independent gradient noise averaged at the server, but local updates also change where gradients are evaluated before aggregation. For nonlinear objectives, these local fluctuations can affect the mean as well as the variance. Which bias contributions are reduced by client averaging, and how do local updates and persistent controls shape those that remain?
 
-This distinction matters for averaged models. Averaging stationary iterates preserves their expected parameters even when it reduces variance. A small squared-bias share of single-checkpoint error therefore need not remain small after averaging. Understanding this error requires identifying how the mean shift is generated, not only the fluctuations around it.
+Client averaging at the server is distinct from temporal averaging of successive global models across communication rounds. Temporal averaging preserves the expected parameter under stationarity even as it reduces variance. Thus a small squared-bias share of single-checkpoint error need not remain small for an averaged output. Understanding this difference requires identifying how the systematic mean shift is generated, not only the fluctuations around it.
 
 Recent stationary analyses provide a starting point. In the homogeneous setting, \citet[Theorem~5.3]{mangold2025scaffold} identify a leading \Scaf{} mean-bias contribution proportional to $\gamma/N$, where $\gamma$ is the step size and $N$ the number of clients, and point to higher-order bias not suppressed by client averaging. This expansion and the \FedAvg{} result of \citet[Theorem~3]{mangold2025fedavg} leave the client-independent $\gamma^2$ coefficient within the remainder. They therefore do not determine whether the two algorithms have the same coefficient at this order, or how much is associated with persistent controls. Section~\ref{sec:related} compares their precise statements with ours.
 
